WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.467
-Welcome to the show. Thanks for coming on.

00:00:02.467 --> 00:00:04.767
-Thank you for having me. -Jesse, you --

00:00:04.767 --> 00:00:07.100
I know you're married to a good friend of ours.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.968
I love her so much -- Kirsten Dunst,

00:00:08.968 --> 00:00:11.200
a very talented actress. [ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.801
-Yes, yes.

00:00:12.801 --> 00:00:15.601
-And you have two little, little babies.

00:00:15.601 --> 00:00:17.334
How old are the kids?

00:00:17.334 --> 00:00:19.167
-Our oldest is almost 5.

00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:22.734
Our youngest just turned 2 a few days ago.

00:00:22.734 --> 00:00:24.167
-So cute.

00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:29.000
-And actually, the main reason for me coming and doing the show

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.133
is I have something that I want to talk to you about.

00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:34.434
-Sure. -I want to thank you.

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:36.267
-You want to thank me? -I want to thank you.

00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:38.067
And I also have a bone to pick with you.

00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:40.400
-Oh. -Are you guys familiar with --

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:44.000
with Jimmy's children's book work?

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.200
[ Cheers ] -Of course.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:46.467
-Writing. Anyways...

00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:47.701
[ Laughter ]

00:00:47.701 --> 00:00:50.567
He's a very well-renowned children's book author.

00:00:50.567 --> 00:00:52.501
-I'm like Dr. Seuss. Yeah, of course.

00:00:52.501 --> 00:00:54.868
-In his free time. [ Laughter ]

00:00:54.868 --> 00:00:57.501
-But, no, Kirsten was on the show a while ago.

00:00:57.501 --> 00:01:01.634
This was, I think, before our oldest was born.

00:01:01.634 --> 00:01:04.234
And you sent a couple of your books --

00:01:04.234 --> 00:01:06.133
"Your Baby's First Word Will be Mama"...

00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:07.667
-Yes. -...and the Dada book.

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:09.067
-Yes, that's right.

00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:12.767
-And they provided some, you know, great times for us.

00:01:12.767 --> 00:01:15.234
We really enjoyed them.

00:01:15.234 --> 00:01:17.334
And they're for young kids, right?

00:01:17.334 --> 00:01:19.133
Wouldn't you say? -Yeah. They're -- Yeah.

00:01:19.133 --> 00:01:22.868
-And our kid is -- our kid is 5 now.

00:01:22.868 --> 00:01:27.167
And all he wants to read is Mama and Dada book.

00:01:27.167 --> 00:01:28.868
[ Laughter ] -Yeah.

00:01:28.868 --> 00:01:30.400
I think that's fantastic.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:31.801
[ Laughter ] -No, it is.

00:01:31.801 --> 00:01:33.868
-But he's 5. -He's 5.

00:01:33.868 --> 00:01:35.701
-Yeah, he's probably -- -And now I'm worried about,

00:01:35.701 --> 00:01:37.734
you know... [ Laughter ]

00:01:37.734 --> 00:01:40.400
-Yeah. Yeah. He needs to take another...

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.667
-Yeah. -Another level.

00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:42.868
Take it to another level.

00:01:42.868 --> 00:01:45.100
No, the Dada books were great.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:46.767
[ Laughter ]

00:01:46.767 --> 00:01:49.267
But lookit, lookit. You get through them real quick.

00:01:49.267 --> 00:01:50.534
You get through the books real quick.

00:01:50.534 --> 00:01:51.801
-Yeah. [ Laughter ]

00:01:51.801 --> 00:01:54.667
And as a way to just kind of entertain ourselves,

00:01:54.667 --> 00:01:57.167
there's a little bit in the back where there's a --

00:01:57.167 --> 00:01:59.567
all the parents picking up the kids from school.

00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:01.367
And so we, like, started improvising...

00:02:01.367 --> 00:02:03.634
-You really did read the book.

00:02:03.634 --> 00:02:06.234
-Like thousands of times, yes. [ Laughter ]

00:02:06.234 --> 00:02:08.367
-All right. Wait. I kind of know the bone you're about to pick.

00:02:08.367 --> 00:02:10.200
Yeah. You -- You're tired of the book.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.033
-I'm tired of the book. -Yeah, I understand.

00:02:12.033 --> 00:02:14.033
Yeah, yeah, I understand. That's why I wrote this book.

00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:16.067
Because I was reading the same books

00:02:16.067 --> 00:02:17.167
you always read to the kids,

00:02:17.167 --> 00:02:18.667
and you go, "I can't read it again.

00:02:18.667 --> 00:02:19.934
I'm gonna go crazy."

00:02:19.934 --> 00:02:22.767
-It's the equivalent of "Baby Shark," this book.

00:02:22.767 --> 00:02:24.767
It's like, you -- [ Laughter ]

00:02:24.767 --> 00:02:27.267
-No, no, no, no! -No, in a good way.

00:02:27.267 --> 00:02:28.467
-Oh, okay. -In a good way.

00:02:28.467 --> 00:02:32.300
It's like he cracked some child's mind code.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.234
-That was -- I got very lucky with that,

00:02:34.234 --> 00:02:36.000
with that book. And a lot of kids --

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.100
-Please write another book. Please.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.767
-I actually -- I actually did.

00:02:39.767 --> 00:02:41.801
And I want to give it to you. This is...

00:02:41.801 --> 00:02:43.767
Do you have this? I know...

00:02:43.767 --> 00:02:45.133
-"Nana"? -"Nana."

00:02:45.133 --> 00:02:46.367
"Nana Loves You More."

00:02:46.367 --> 00:02:47.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:47.534 --> 00:02:50.033
This one...

00:02:50.033 --> 00:02:52.300
This one -- This is the one.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:53.934
-I'm excited, and I'm a little scared.

00:02:53.934 --> 00:02:56.167
-No, no, I know -- I know Kirsten's mom,

00:02:56.167 --> 00:02:57.901
and so I think you're gonna love this.

00:02:57.901 --> 00:02:59.267
And she's gonna love this. This is a good one.

00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:01.234
There's more words. -That's great.

00:03:01.234 --> 00:03:02.667
[ Laughter ] -It's more -- It's more words.

00:03:02.667 --> 00:03:04.767
Yeah. I'm taking babies -- We're getting bigger and bigger.

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:06.234
I'm writing more and more. -All right.

00:03:06.234 --> 00:03:09.067
-Yeah. I want you to come on every year and see my progress.

00:03:09.067 --> 00:03:10.234
-Yeah, okay, great. [ Laughter ]

00:03:10.234 --> 00:03:11.968
-Thank you, Jesse. I appreciate that.

00:03:11.968 --> 00:03:15.000
I want to talk about you because I'm a giant fan of yours.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.334
So, I've been wanting to have you on the show for a long time.

00:03:18.334 --> 00:03:20.767
Last year, you and Kirsten got Oscar nominated,

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:24.400
by the way, for the same movie. Congratulations on that.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:27.801
"Power of the Dog."

00:03:27.801 --> 00:03:30.200
"Power of the Dog." -Is that fun

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.734
when you go with your wife to the Oscars.

00:03:31.734 --> 00:03:32.901
You're like, "Hey, we're both nominated.

00:03:32.901 --> 00:03:35.868
This is kind of cool." -It's very surreal.

00:03:35.868 --> 00:03:38.434
But -- but, yeah, I mean, it's something --

00:03:38.434 --> 00:03:39.734
It was so unexpected.

00:03:39.734 --> 00:03:43.267
I knew the odds for Kirsten and Benedict and Kodi

00:03:43.267 --> 00:03:45.100
were pretty high for them to get nominated

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:47.934
and some of the other cast and crew.

00:03:47.934 --> 00:03:49.834
But the last thing I expected

00:03:49.834 --> 00:03:52.534
was to be a part of that list, as well.

00:03:52.534 --> 00:03:55.968
And we had our family there.

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:57.667
It was just -- Yeah, it was unbelievably special.

00:03:57.667 --> 00:03:59.667
-Who from the family went?

00:03:59.667 --> 00:04:01.467
-Pretty much the whole crew.

00:04:01.467 --> 00:04:04.434
But, yeah, my dad,

00:04:04.434 --> 00:04:08.000
I've realized, mainly through that award season,

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.567
is that he has a real knack, probably better than I am,

00:04:11.567 --> 00:04:14.667
at, like, cruising parties and...

00:04:14.667 --> 00:04:16.300
-Schmooze? -His name is Jim Bob.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:18.334
He's a retired fireman, cowboy...

00:04:18.334 --> 00:04:20.767
-His name is Jim Bob? [ Laughter ]

00:04:20.767 --> 00:04:22.567
-I'm surprised. I wish he was here.

00:04:22.567 --> 00:04:24.400
-Jim Bob, yeah!

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:25.767
That's awesome. [ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:25.767 --> 00:04:27.467
And so he went and cruised the party?

00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:33.267
-I mean, yeah, he -- he -- There's no one that he meets

00:04:33.267 --> 00:04:35.200
that isn't his best friend in a few minutes.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.901
He's, like, introducing me to everyone.

00:04:37.901 --> 00:04:40.767
-Yeah. It sounds like -- He's from Texas, as well?

00:04:40.767 --> 00:04:42.601
-Yeah. A little town in Central Texas.

00:04:42.601 --> 00:04:44.367
-Yeah. Come on. Jim Bob from Texas.

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:46.334
That's charming. Come on. Exactly right.

00:04:46.334 --> 00:04:48.667
Well, he's got to be proud of you, bud.

00:04:48.667 --> 00:04:50.133
Let's talk about this new limited series,

00:04:50.133 --> 00:04:51.467
"Love &amp; Death."

00:04:51.467 --> 00:04:53.968
It's you and Elizabeth Olsen, and it's just crusher.

00:04:53.968 --> 00:04:55.133
Is it David E. Kelley.

00:04:55.133 --> 00:04:58.067
-David E. Kelley. Directed by Lesli Linka Glatter.

00:04:58.067 --> 00:05:00.367
-Just crushes, man.

00:05:00.367 --> 00:05:04.434
This is a bizarre story. It's a true story.

00:05:04.434 --> 00:05:06.234
And do you want to explain

00:05:06.234 --> 00:05:10.234
to everyone what the love and death part maybe?

00:05:10.234 --> 00:05:11.567
-Yeah. [ Laughter ]

00:05:11.567 --> 00:05:13.534
Well, we sort of let you know right out of the gate

00:05:13.534 --> 00:05:16.434
what you're getting into. There's a --

00:05:16.434 --> 00:05:18.701
It -- As Jimmy said, it takes place --

00:05:18.701 --> 00:05:22.667
It's a true story. It takes place in the late '70s

00:05:22.667 --> 00:05:23.968
in a suburb of Dallas.

00:05:23.968 --> 00:05:26.267
These, you know, very family-oriented,

00:05:26.267 --> 00:05:29.834
churchgoing folks that are kind of all

00:05:29.834 --> 00:05:32.400
chasing the American dream, and on the surface,

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:37.267
things seem to be working out well.

00:05:37.267 --> 00:05:41.133
And my character, Allan Gore,

00:05:41.133 --> 00:05:44.501
is friends with this -- with Lizzie's character.

00:05:44.501 --> 00:05:48.734
And they play -- they play in this church volleyball league.

00:05:48.734 --> 00:05:52.534
And at a certain point, they're both going for the same ball.

00:05:52.534 --> 00:05:57.801
And Lizzie's character catches my character's scent

00:05:57.801 --> 00:06:00.300
and in that moment thinks, "You know what?

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.701
I would like to have an affair with that guy."

00:06:02.701 --> 00:06:04.300
And, um... [ Laughter ]

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:06.701
And then that's how the show...

00:06:06.701 --> 00:06:08.100
-That's the -- that's the love part.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:09.267
-That's the love part.

00:06:09.267 --> 00:06:10.634
And then, yeah.

00:06:10.634 --> 00:06:12.501
You have to watch to find out the other part.

00:06:12.501 --> 00:06:14.267
[ Laughter ] -Yeah.

00:06:14.267 --> 00:06:16.334
You have to watch to find out the other part.

00:06:16.334 --> 00:06:18.501
You can google it, too. -Yes.

00:06:18.501 --> 00:06:20.200
It's a very well-known story. -It's a well-known story.

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:24.501
It's an insane story, but well -- well-acted.

00:06:24.501 --> 00:06:27.267
And a different take on --

00:06:27.267 --> 00:06:30.133
You get to see the -- from all different angles

00:06:30.133 --> 00:06:31.234
of the different characters.

00:06:31.234 --> 00:06:32.767
You go, "Oh, that's interesting."

00:06:32.767 --> 00:06:36.434
-Well, yeah, it's something that even just shooting it,

00:06:36.434 --> 00:06:39.534
when you first read the story, your immediate reaction --

00:06:39.534 --> 00:06:41.667
my immediate reaction -- was was one thing.

00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:45.067
And as it goes along, you know, you realize

00:06:45.067 --> 00:06:48.767
that there's not really any one particular character

00:06:48.767 --> 00:06:50.901
you can point the finger at or blame

00:06:50.901 --> 00:06:55.501
or that's the antagonist. So it's just an interesting

00:06:55.501 --> 00:06:58.334
character study of these very, you know --

00:06:58.334 --> 00:07:00.901
I don't know -- small-town, relatable,

00:07:00.901 --> 00:07:04.667
real people going through unimaginable sort of things.

00:07:04.667 --> 00:07:07.033
You know? -Before we show a clip,

00:07:07.033 --> 00:07:08.601
I just want to say, I think I want to say

00:07:08.601 --> 00:07:12.400
that you were in maybe 6 or 7 movies

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:14.234
that were nominated for Oscars.

00:07:14.234 --> 00:07:16.501
And I think there's a reason for that.

00:07:16.501 --> 00:07:18.300
You're fantastic, buddy.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:20.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:20.634 --> 00:07:22.467
I want to show a clip.

00:07:22.467 --> 00:07:24.701
Here's Jesse Plemmons and Elizabeth Olsen

00:07:24.701 --> 00:07:27.467
in "Love &amp; Death." Take a look at this.

00:07:27.467 --> 00:07:29.400
-I would never be able to forgive myself

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:33.133
if Betty ever found out.

00:07:33.133 --> 00:07:36.467
That would just be devastating to her.

00:07:36.467 --> 00:07:40.133
-I feel the same. We would have to be so careful

00:07:40.133 --> 00:07:44.267
that no one would ever know except us.

00:07:44.267 --> 00:07:46.968
-That's right.

00:07:46.968 --> 00:07:50.334
-I've been thinking a lot about what you said about...

00:07:50.334 --> 00:07:51.667
just wanting to go to bed,

00:07:51.667 --> 00:07:57.868
not getting too emotionally involved and so forth.

00:07:57.868 --> 00:08:00.200
That would be very important to me.

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:01.367
-Me too, Allan.

00:08:01.367 --> 00:08:03.767
♪♪

00:08:03.767 --> 00:08:05.767
God, I just want to enjoy myself.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:13.868
Without hurting myself or anyone else.

00:08:13.868 --> 00:08:15.968
-Whoa! [ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:15.968 --> 00:08:17.601
Jesse Plemmons, everybody!

00:08:17.601 --> 00:08:22.801
"Love &amp; Death" debuts April 27th on HBO Max!

00:08:22.801 --> 00:08:24.033
More "Tonight Show" after the break.

00:08:24.033 --> 00:08:25.667
Stick around, everybody.

